<https://hdl.handle.net/10665/252183> <https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/dspace#dc.rights.uri> "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/igo" <https://iris.who.int/graph/community/5bff2c18-6c2b-4658-9087-93811d2399c0> .
<https://hdl.handle.net/10665/259360> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract> "This handbook introduces the National Health Workforce Accounts (NHWA), a standardized framework developed by the World Health Organization to strengthen health workforce information systems and support evidence-based workforce planning and policy-making. The publication is intended to assist countries in monitoring and analysing health workforce capacity, distribution, education, labour market dynamics, financing, governance and information systems in the context of universal health coverage and the health-related Sustainable Development Goals. It outlines the rationale for improved health workforce data and highlights the role of harmonized metrics in addressing challenges related to workforce availability, accessibility, performance and retention.\n\nThe handbook presents a modular framework comprising 78 core indicators organized across 10 modules covering active workforce stock, education and training, regulation and accreditation, education financing, labour market flows, employment conditions, workforce spending and remuneration, skill mix, governance, and health workforce information systems. For each indicator, it provides definitions, methodological guidance, reporting requirements, data sources and disaggregation options. The publication also describes implementation principles, reporting standards and potential applications of the NHWA, supporting countries in strengthening health workforce monitoring, facilitating international comparability and informing policies aimed at improving health system performance and population access to quality health services." <https://iris.who.int/graph/community/5bff2c18-6c2b-4658-9087-93811d2399c0> .
